  different security properties.

  [1mThe fork:[0m at the moment [38;5;153mlogin[39m is called, nothing in this app knows a user ID. The form collects a username and
  password; the ID that identifies the account is something the [3mserver[0m would assign after authenticating. So "a userId
  parameter so we can track who logged in" can mean three different things, and they aren't interchangeable.

  - [1mServer returns it[0m — [38;5;153mlogin[39m keeps its signature and the identity comes back in the result: [38;5;153m{ success, user, userId }[39m.
    Buys you real tracking of who logged in, with the ID authenticated. Costs nothing at the call site. Rules out
    nothing. This is the standard shape.
  - [1mClient-generated correlation ID parameter[0m — [38;5;153mlogin(username, password, requestId)[39m where the caller mints a random ID
    per attempt. Buys you the ability to trace a login attempt through logs even when it fails. It is [3mnot[0m an identity —
    you still don't know who logged in until the server answers. Cheap to add later or remove.
  - [1mCaller-supplied identity parameter[0m — [38;5;153mlogin(userId, username, password)[39m with the caller asserting who the user is.
    This is the literal reading of your request, and I'd push back on it: a client-supplied identity that the auth path
    trusts is the shape of an auth bypass. It only makes sense if some upstream system already authenticated the user
    and this stub is downstream of that — which isn't the case in this repo today.

  [1mMy recommendation: the first.[0m It's the only one that actually answers "who logged in" with a trustworthy value, and it
  doesn't change the signature other code depends on. If you also want failed attempts traceable, the correlation ID
  composes with it — they aren't exclusive.
