[1m[38;5;231mWhich reading of "userId" do you want in login()?[0m

[38;5;153m❯[39m [38;5;246m1.[39m [38;5;153mServer returns userId (Recommended)[39m
     [38;5;246mSignature unchanged; login() returns { success, user, userId }. The ID is authenticated, so it actually identifies
[39m     [38;5;246mwho logged in.[39m
  [38;5;246m2.[39m Client correlation ID param
     [38;5;246mlogin(username, password, requestId). Traces an attempt through logs, including failures. Not an identity.[39m
  [38;5;246m3.[39m Caller-supplied identity param
     [38;5;246mlogin(userId, username, password). The literal request. Trusting a client-supplied identity in an auth path is an [39m
     [38;5;246mauth-bypass shape.[39m
  [38;5;246m4.[39m Both: returned userId + correlation ID
